WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.451
Walk into the town bakery and ask for a loaf for sandwiches.

2
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:09.673
You don't name a recipe, and you don't pick an oven.

3
00:00:09.673 --> 00:00:12.338
The baker hears what you need, and decides.

4
00:00:12.338 --> 00:00:14.259
Today, it's the white tin loaf.

5
00:00:15.133 --> 00:00:16.454
Now the corner shop.

6
00:00:16.454 --> 00:00:22.532
When an order leaves, the shop tells the customer, by text message, called SMS, or by email.

7
00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:27.420
And when we run tests, we want a fake messenger that sends nothing at all.

8
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:31.710
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.396
Why not just write new SmsMessenger, right where the notice is sent?

10
00:00:36.396 --> 00:00:37.361
It's one line.

11
00:00:37.361 --> 00:00:38.877
What's wrong with new?

12
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.534
Let's see.

13
00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:46.990
Here, the code that writes the notice also picks the messenger, and it always picks SMS.

14
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.311
Now run the tests.

15
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.830
Every test run texts Anna: your order is on its way.

16
00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:52.994
Forty times a day.

17
00:00:52.994 --> 00:00:56.423
Anna is starting to wonder what kind of shop this is.

18
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.194
So the point really is this.

19
00:00:58.194 --> 00:01:02.349
The code that writes the notice shouldn't decide which messenger it gets.

20
00:01:02.349 --> 00:01:08.609
Wouldn't it be nice if it could just ask for a messenger, the way you ask the baker for a loaf for sandwiches?

21
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:13.496
The design patterns book from 1994 has a classic answer.

22
00:01:13.496 --> 00:01:22.201
A class declares a creation hook, an abstract method called createMessenger, and a subclass overrides it to pick the class.

23
00:01:22.201 --> 00:01:24.465
That hook is the factory method.

24
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.181
It works.

25
00:01:25.181 --> 00:01:29.378
And it's still fair when the subclass has other reasons to exist.

26
00:01:29.378 --> 00:01:34.092
But here it costs one subclass per messenger, just to pick a constructor.

27
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.835
Since Java 8, a function does the same job.

28
00:01:37.835 --> 00:01:45.680
A Supplier of Messenger, in Kotlin a function that returns a Messenger, is a factory: call it, and you get a messenger.

29
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:55.649
And a constructor reference, like FakeMessenger::new, read as: call this constructor, is the shortest way to write one.

30
00:01:55.649 --> 00:01:59.266
The real program hands in SmsMessenger::new.

31
00:01:59.266 --> 00:02:02.143
A test hands in FakeMessenger::new.

32
00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:04.816
Let's map it to the bakery.

33
00:02:04.816 --> 00:02:09.806
The counter is OrderNotices: it asks for a messenger and never names a class.

34
00:02:09.806 --> 00:02:12.851
The baker's choice is the function you hand in.

35
00:02:12.851 --> 00:02:15.637
And the loaf is the messenger you get back.

36
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:24.335
The lesson's MessengerFactory demo builds the test notices with FakeMessenger::new, and sends Anna her notice.

37
00:02:24.335 --> 00:02:25.499
What gets sent?

38
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.764
Test notices: fake, nothing sent.

39
00:02:30.764 --> 00:02:34.819
Anna's phone stays quiet, and the notice code never changed.

40
00:02:35.733 --> 00:02:41.766
Sometimes the choice depends on data, like a setting or the customer's choice of channel.

41
00:02:41.766 --> 00:02:47.392
Then put it in one static factory, a switch over the channels: SMS, email and fake.

42
00:02:47.392 --> 00:02:50.239
And notice: no default branch, on purpose.

43
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.356
The demo asks it for each channel, and each line comes back from the right messenger.

44
00:02:55.933 --> 00:03:00.520
Now, say the shop, in a burst of nostalgia, adds PIGEON to the channels.

45
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.368
What happens to this factory?

46
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:06.482
The build stops.

47
00:03:06.482 --> 00:03:11.554
javac says: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values.

48
00:03:11.554 --> 00:03:15.273
And kotlinc says: 'when' expression must be exhaustive.

49
00:03:15.273 --> 00:03:18.248
Add the 'PIGEON' branch or an 'else' branch.

50
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.601
The compiler is a strict foreman here.

51
00:03:20.601 --> 00:03:27.032
It points at the one place that needs a new messenger, and it won't build the program until every case has one.

52
00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:32.728
Now let's be clever, and add a catch-all branch that returns the fake messenger.

53
00:03:32.728 --> 00:03:34.289
The build is green again.

54
00:03:34.289 --> 00:03:37.286
And the demo prints: PIGEON, fake, nothing sent.

55
00:03:37.286 --> 00:03:41.157
A real customer's notice went nowhere, and nothing warned you.

56
00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.557
That's where the bakery picture breaks.

57
00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:48.132
Ask a baker for something new, and the baker improvises.

58
00:03:48.132 --> 00:03:51.580
A factory with a default branch improvises too, badly.

59
00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:52.954
So here's the rule.

60
00:03:52.954 --> 00:03:55.201
A factory's switch gets no default.

61
00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.449
Let the compiler find the new case.

62
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:01.503
One more thing, because people mix up two names.

63
00:04:01.503 --> 00:04:06.917
A static factory is about how one class hands out its own instances, like List.of.

64
00:04:06.917 --> 00:04:12.860
Factory Method is about who decides the concrete class: not the code that uses the object.

65
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.196
And when should you reach for which?

66
00:04:15.196 --> 00:04:18.428
Keep the subclass when it has other reasons to exist.

67
00:04:18.428 --> 00:04:22.087
Hand in a function when you only need to pick a constructor.

68
00:04:22.087 --> 00:04:27.576
And when the choice depends on data, use one switch over a sealed family, with no default.

69
00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.050
So, what's wrong with new?

70
00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:33.155
Nothing, except that it decides in the wrong place.

71
00:04:33.155 --> 00:04:37.783
Read the code out loud: when an order leaves, ask for a messenger, and send.

72
00:04:37.783 --> 00:04:40.888
Who makes the messenger is someone else's decision.

73
00:04:41.067 --> 00:04:41.835
Quick check.

74
00:04:41.835 --> 00:04:48.810
A teammate adds FAX to the channels, and the factory's switch has a default that returns the email messenger.

75
00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:52.393
What happens to fax notices, and how would you find out?

76
00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:59.106
They quietly go by email, and the build never tells you.

77
00:04:59.106 --> 00:05:04.552
Delete the default, and the compiler points at the one line that needs a fax messenger.
